    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d251">

                <pb facs="0446" n="188" xml:id="pg188"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">File No. 763.72112/1003a</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to the
                        Ambassador Great Britain.</hi> (<hi
                            rendition="#italic">Page</hi>)</head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#center">[Telegram]</seg>
                </opener>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Washington</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1915-03-03T19:00:00-05:00">
                            <hi rendition="#italic">March, 3, 1915, 7 p. m.</hi>
                        </date>
                    </dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>1219. Following telegram being sent by Spring
                        Rice to Grey:<anchor n="1"
                        xml:id="d251-f1"/>
                </p>
                <quote rend="blockquote">
                    <p>Contracts for cotton made before issuance of declaration<anchor n="2"
                            xml:id="d251-f2"/> in good faith and in view of assurance that cotton
                        would not be treated as contraband ask permission to fill existing contracts
                        by delivery of cotton to German ports. If permission is not granted,
                        information is asked for as to treatment of cotton already bought and
                        freight paid for and to be loaded on board named ships which although
                        chartered have not yet arrived at American ports owing to unforeseen
                        delays.</p>
                    <p>Owners would be liable to incur heavy loss unless assurance is given at once
                        as to rate of compensation for cargo consigned to Germany which will now
                        under declaration be detained or diverted. Owners assume that compensation
                        will amount to invoice price less cost of freight from Danish and Swedish
                        ports to which freight has already been paid. Ships are <hi
                            rendition="#italic">Marie, Dicido</hi>, Swedish, and <hi
                            rendition="#italic">Livonia</hi>, Danish, from Galveston to Aalborg and
                        Copenhagen.</p>
                </quote>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Bryan</hi>
                        
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d251-f1" n="1" xml:id="d251-f1-content">
                <p>This telegram was sent in consequence of representations made personally to the
                    British Ambassador by Robert F. Rose,
                    Foreign Trade Adviser of the Department of State, accompanied by an exporter of
                    cotton, in a conference at the Department on the same date. See Mr. Rose’s report dated May 21, <hi
                        rendition="#italic">post</hi>, <ref target="#pg216">p. 216</ref>.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d251-f2" n="2" xml:id="d251-f2-content">
                <p>The declaration of March 1 prohibiting trade with Germany, <hi
                        rendition="#italic">ante</hi>, <ref target="#pg127">p. 127</ref>.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
